---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="History of Hashing | T2H3">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-10">
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-[2rem] border border-red-700/40 bg-black shadow-2xl">
      <div class="bg-[url('/images/dark-banner.jpg')] bg-cover bg-center">
        <div class="bg-black/80 p-10">
          <p class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-red-500">
            Hash House Harriers History
          </p>
          <h1 class="mt-3 text-5xl font-black text-white">
            From Hare and Hounds to Hashing Around the World
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-zinc-300">
            Hashing grew from the old British paper-chase tradition into a worldwide
            social activity built around trails, fellowship, humour and refreshments.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6">
      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-red-600/30 bg-zinc-900 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-red-500">The Roots: Hare and Hounds</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          Long before the Hash House Harriers existed, British runners took part in
          paper chases, also known as Hare and Hounds. A hare laid a trail using paper,
          and a pack of hounds followed it across the countryside. Harrier clubs grew
          from this tradition during the nineteenth century, combining cross-country
          exercise with the thrill of following a trail.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">1938: The First Hash</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          In 1938, a group of British expatriates in Kuala Lumpur, Malaya, formed the
          original Hash House Harriers. Alberto Esteban Ignacio Gispert, known as
          &ldquo;G,&rdquo; is generally credited as the founder, alongside a small group
          of fellow runners. Their club mixed the trail-running traditions of harrier
          clubs with a deliberately social and non-competitive atmosphere.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          The name came from the nickname of their dining club at the Selangor Club
          Annex: the &ldquo;Hash House.&rdquo; The original Kuala Lumpur kennel later became
          known throughout the hashing world as Mother Hash.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">War, Suspension and Rebirth</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          Mother Hash completed its 100th run in August 1941. Hashing was suspended in
          December 1941 during the Second World War and resumed in Kuala Lumpur in
          August 1946. The simple format survived: one or more hares mark a trail, and
          the pack works together to find and follow it.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">The Original Objectives</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          When the Kuala Lumpur club formalized its registration in the early 1950s,
          its famously light-hearted objectives were to promote fitness, cure weekend
          hangovers, build a thirst and satisfy it with beer, and remind older members
          that they were not as old as they felt.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">Hashing Spreads Worldwide</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          The first verified branch kennel was established in Singapore in 1962.
          Hashing then spread through Asia and onward to Europe, Australia, Africa,
          North America, South America and even Antarctica. Each kennel developed its
          own customs, but the basic idea remained recognizable everywhere: follow the
          trail, enjoy the circle and do not take the competition too seriously.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-2xl border border-red-600/30 bg-black p-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-red-500">The Tradition Today</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-300">
          Modern trails may be laid with flour, chalk, paper or other markings and can
          wind through countryside, city streets, alleys and parks. Packs include
          runners, joggers and walkers. What connects kennels around the world is the
          combination of trail finding, fellowship, songs, humour and refreshments.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-zinc-400">
          Historical reference: Ed &ldquo;Hazukashii&rdquo; Howell&rsquo;s
          <a
            href="https://www.gotothehash.net/history/hhhhistory.html"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="font-bold text-red-500 underline decoration-red-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-white"
          >A Historical Review of the Hash House Harriers</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
